.TH ATERM 1 "2026-10-15" "aterm" "aterm Manual"
.SH NAME
aterm \- a transparent, introspecting terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B aterm
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B aterm \-\-window
.RI [ WINDOW\-OPTIONS ]
.br
.B aterm \-\-headless
.RI [ WINDOW\-OPTIONS ]
.br
.B aterm ctl
.RB [ \-\-sock
.I PATH
|
.B \-\-pid
.IR PID ]
.RB [ \-\-timeout
.IR SECS ]
.RI [ @<sel> ]
.I verb
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B aterm
.I verb
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B aterm
.I tool
.RI [ args... ]
.br
.B aterm help
.RI [ topic ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B aterm
is one binary. With a TTY it is the
.IR session :
it spawns your
.B $SHELL
in a PTY and passes I/O through unchanged; the host terminal draws the bytes,
and the session keeps no screen state and serves no control socket. With no
TTY,
.B \-\-window
or
.B \-\-headless
it is the
.IR window :
a GPU terminal (Metal on macOS, OpenGL on Linux, Direct3D 11 on Windows,
with a CPU fallback) or,
headless, the same engine, PTYs and control socket with no window.
.B aterm ctl
drives the window through that socket.
.PP
The names
.BR aterm\-gui ,
.BR aterm\-cli ,
.BR aterm\-ctl ,
.BR aterm\-fleet ,
.BR aterm\-drive ,
.B aterm\-link
and
.B atpkg
are links to this binary, dispatched by
.IR argv[0] .
.PP
In the
.B user
(default) and
.B master
containment modes the shell inherits the launching shell's limits. In
.B safety
it gets soft
.BR setrlimit (2)
caps (8192 open files; 16 GiB address space off macOS), which its rc can
raise, or, on Windows, Job Object limits.
.B containment
adds the macOS sandbox (Seatbelt) and sets those caps as hard limits, which
the program cannot raise; it refuses to start where there is no OS sandbox.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-window
Open the GPU window (a launch with no TTY, such as from Finder, opens it too).
.TP
.B \-\-headless
Serve the engine and control socket with no window (announced on stderr).
.TP
.B \-\-session
Force the transparent shell session (piped or CI runs).
.TP
.BI \-\-containment " MODE"
.BR master ", " user ", " safety " or " containment
(case\-insensitive; the last flag wins). An invalid value fails closed to
.BR containment .
.B \-\-sandbox
and
.B \-\-no\-sandbox
are shorthands for
.B containment
and
.BR user .
.IP
The sandbox allows no network, no debugger attach, writes only to temp dirs
and shell history, no access to credential stores or private data, and no
mach service lookup but user and group queries, so
.BR open ,
.BR osascript ,
.B pbcopy
and the keychain do nothing. The window brokers nothing for a contained tab:
no OSC 52 clipboard, no Kitty file, temp\-file or shared\-memory image, no
desktop notification. A plain
.B aterm \-\-sandbox
session parses nothing, so the terminal around it applies its own policy to
the program's escape sequences (an aterm window in
.B user
mode writes the pasteboard from its OSC 52).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reroute
Restore the upstream Rust names (cargo, rustc, ...) in this session; see
.BR "aterm help reroute" .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Hide the session's startup line.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Session: a one\-line summary on exit. Window: routine startup notices.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Print help / version and exit.
.SS Window options
Run
.B aterm \-\-window \-\-help
for the complete set and the config keys. Precedence is flag > config >
default.
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-command " cmd [args...]"
Run
.I cmd
instead of
.BR $SHELL ;
consumes the rest of the command line.
.B \-\-hold
keeps the window open after it exits.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-working\-directory " dir"
Start in
.IR dir .
.TP
.BI \-\-control\-sock " path"
Bind the control socket at
.I path
.RB ( 0 / off
or
.B \-\-no\-control\-sock
disables it).
.TP
.BI \-\-columns " n" ", " \-\-lines " n"
Initial grid size.
.TP
.BR \-\-gpu ", " \-\-cpu
Force the renderer for this launch (the last one wins).
.TP
.BI \-\-scale " f"
Force the render scale; headless, the
.B image
capture renders at that DPI.
.TP
.BI \-\-font\-px " n"
Font size in physical pixels.
.TP
.BI \-\-shell " name|path"
The interactive shell to spawn.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-shell\-integration
Disable the shell\-integration marks.
.TP
.B \-\-diagnose
Print a diagnostics report and exit.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
Each runs without spawning a shell:
.BR show\-config ,
.BR explain\-config ,
.B doctor
(fixes what it can unless
.BR \-\-read\-only ;
exits 1 only when the shell cannot start),
.BR list\-fonts ,
.BI show\-face " family" ,
.BR list\-themes ,
.BR list\-kitty\-commands ,
.BR exceptions " [" \-\-all ].
.SH VERBS
.BI "aterm help " verb
has each one's page.
.TP
.B aterm ctl \fIargs\fR
Read and drive an aterm window's sessions over its control socket (below).
.TP
.B aterm conn \fR[\fIcmd\fR]
Standing pull/push wiring between sessions
.RB ( ls / add / set / rm / spawn / show / map ).
.TP
.B aterm pkg \fIargs\fR
Install, update and verify the toolchain (the package manager).
.TP
.B aterm \fItool\fR [\fIargs\fR]
Run an installed toolchain program from the managed store, never
.BR $PATH ;
.BI "aterm pkg install " tool
adds one.
.TP
.B aterm fleet \fIargs\fR
This machine's place in a fleet, the machines that
.B aterm ctl survey \-\-fleet
reads
.RI ( init " | " join " | " peers " | " leave ).
.TP
.B aterm drive \fIargs\fR
The window harness's readers by hand
.RI ( classify " | " phase " | " answer " | " ledger ).
.TP
.BR "aterm link" ", " "aterm fabric"
Mail between this machine's sessions and people (the fabric bus).
.TP
.B aterm fabric brief \fR[\fB@\fIsid\fR[\fB@\fInode\fR]] [\fB\-\-window\fR \fIrecords\fR] [\fB\-\-budget\fR \fItokens\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR]
The fleet's session logs folded from the bus alone (no control socket): each
session's open run and turn, its needs and recent turns, marked
.B partial
when the window holds neither its start nor a snapshot
.RB ( why=no-start ),
when it lost records no snapshot it holds folds past
.RB ( why=lost ),
when its node went away or relaunched before its close reached the bus
.RB ( why=node-gone ),
or when its own presence says it exited without that close
.RB ( why=no-close ),
and
.B holes=
for loss nobody said, from the first record the window reaches. The whole is
held to
.B \-\-budget
tokens; the sessions past it are counted
.BR more=sessions: .
.B \-\-json
prints the same fold as one JSON object for a program: one object per
session, of ids, closed words and counts only (its node and face, the need
word and its detail, the open path's levels and kinds, its mail counts, its
last seq and how many seconds old its newest record is), held to the same
budget.
It shows the session logs of a node whose bridge mirrors them: by default
.RB ( "[fabric] log = \(dqauto\(dq" )
once its broker retains them, as
.B aterm link broker
does
.RB ( "aterm ctl fabric status"
says
.B mirror=unretained
where the broker retains nothing);
.B \(dqturns\(dq
mirrors over any broker, and
.B \(dqoff\(dq
puts nothing of the session logs on the bus.
Each session's turns, runs, needs and the approval, question, wall and stall
steps they rise from go to its
.B ev
face as counts and closed words: no command line, title, cwd, note text or
mail party, and a session that drove another reads as
.BR by=e<8\ hex> ,
never its sid (the hash keyed by a secret the node mints once).
.B aterm fabric tail \-\-bodies
prints such a record's wire fields, labelled by its
.BR src= .
Over a broker that retains its log, a node waits once its records in the
broker's window reach 64 MiB, until the broker gives the oldest up
.RB ( mirror=budget );
with
.B \(dqturns\(dq
over a broker that retains nothing, it stops at 256 MiB of records
.RB ( mirror=capped ).
Closed sessions' records still held for a bridge that no longer mirrors are
said there as
.BR "mirror=unserved tails=" \fIk\fR.
.TP
.B aterm ship \fIargs\fR
Cut and release a build (needs a source checkout).
.TP
.B aterm update \fR[\fIcmd\fR]
Report or check for an update
.RI ( status " | " check " | " identity ).
.TP
.B aterm agents \fR[\fIcmd\fR]
Keep the aterm primer in coding agents' context files.
.TP
.B aterm machines \fIcmd\fR
Configure and manage a lab of Macs from this one over SSH, with no MDM and no
Apple account
.RI ( init " | " scan " | " enroll " | " status " | " plan " | " apply " | " person " | " screen );
macOS only.
.TP
.B aterm harness \fIcmd\fR
Claude Code's spend, limits and accounts, the disk, the approval ledger;
.B upgrade
moves a live Claude Code onto a newer build.
.TP
.B aterm keeper \fIcmd\fR
The PTY keeper (macOS, opt\-in with
.BR "[keeper] enabled = true" ).
.TP
.B aterm acaffiene \fR[\fIcmd\fR]
The menu\-bar keep\-awake mug (macOS), started by every ordinary aterm window.
A click keeps this Mac awake, a right click picks 1, 8 or 24 hours or forever,
Enable at Startup, or Quit. Commands:
.BR start ,
.BR on " [\fIspan\fR],"
.BR off ,
.BR status ,
.BR startup " [\fBon\fR|\fBoff\fR],"
.BR quit ;
see
.BR "aterm help acaffiene" .
.TP
.BR "aterm new\-tab" ", " "aterm new\-window" ", " "aterm split\-pane"
Open a tab, window or split
.RB ( \-d
.IR dir ;
split takes
.BR \-V / \-H ).
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
A window serves a per\-user Unix socket in a mode 0700 directory, speaking
newline\-delimited text. Only same\-uid peers that present the per\-launch
token are served;
.B aterm ctl
presents it. The complete verb list is generated from the engine's verb table:
.B aterm ctl help
(or
.B aterm help introspection
anywhere).
.PP
Without
.B \-\-sock
or
.BR \-\-pid ,
a call from a shell an aterm window or
.B \-\-headless
instance spawned drives the instance hosting the calling terminal
.RB ( $ATERM_PARENT_SESSION_ID );
otherwise
.IR <dir>/aterm.sock ,
the newest instance.
.I <dir>
is
.B $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/aterm
when set, else
.B ~/Library/Application Support/aterm
(every Unix, Linux included) or
.B %TMP%\eaterm
(Windows).
.PP
A leading selector addresses any session of any same\-user instance:
.BI @s\- hex
(a session id from
.BR ls ),
.B @self
(your own),
.B @.\&
(the active tab),
.BI @ n
(a local number), and
.B @*
.RB ( subscribe
only). Verbs group as observe
.RB ( text ", " screen ", " cursor ", " search ", " blocks ),
capture
.RB ( image ", " cast ", " temporal ),
live
.RB ( subscribe ),
drive
.RB ( send ", " key ", " turn ", " resize ", " select ),
wait
.RB ( ready ", " await ", " wait ),
session log
.RB ( log ", " brief ", " mark ,
.B await log
and
.BI "subscribe @" sid " log" ;
a mark is an attributed claim and never acts),
app
.RB ( spawn ", " close ", " tab ", " invoke ", " controls ", " settings ),
connections
.RB ( connect ", " disconnect ", " flows )
and discovery
.RB ( ls ", " instances ", " windows ", " mux ).
.PP
A terminal tab's tooltip and context menu add one
.B now:
line saying what its session log says the session is doing
.RB ( "now: running shell" ", " "now: approval \(pc bash" ", " "now: limit \(pc 14:05" );
while the tab needs you, that comes first, and a label another program set with
.B mark
never shows there.
For 30 seconds after the harness restarts or upgrades an agent, or types its carry-on
after a full disk or a usage limit, the line says so with its author
.RB ( "now: restarting \(pc by the harness" ).
The tab's name is unchanged.
.B "[session_log] clause = false"
turns the line off, and the tooltip and menu are then as before.
.PP
.B aterm ctl
exits 0 on
.BR OK ;
1 on
.BR ERR ,
a connection or usage failure, or, for
.BR ls / instances / windows ,
a socket directory with no instance in it; 2 when
.BR ls / instances / windows
could not look or reach (sockets found but none answered, or the directory
missing, unreadable or unresolvable); 75 when the instance went away
mid\-exchange and nothing replaced it; 124 on a timeout (the client's
.B \-\-timeout
or a server
.BR status=timeout ).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
No environment variable selects a launch choice or overrides an aterm.toml
key;
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
only locates the file (FILES). A shell an aterm window or
.B \-\-headless
instance spawns gets
.BR TERM_PROGRAM=aterm ", " ATERM_CHILD=1 ", " ATERM_PARENT_SESSION_ID ,
.B ATERM_SESSION_ID
and the tool directory on
.BR PATH ;
a plain aterm session gets the tool directory on
.B PATH
but none of those four variables. Every spawned shell has AI\-tool variables
.RB ( CLAUDE* ", " ANTHROPIC_* ", " COPILOT_* ", " CODEX_* ", " ANTIGRAVITY_* ", " GEMINI_* ", " CURSOR_* ", " GROK_* ", " XAI_* ", " KIMI_* ", " MOONSHOT_* ", " AI_* ", " _DEVTOOL_* )
stripped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I <config>/aterm/aterm.toml
The configuration, which the window reloads live.
.I <config>
is
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME
when set, else
.B ~/.config
(macOS, Linux) or
.B %APPDATA%
(Windows);
.B aterm explain\-config
shows the resolution.
.TP
.IR <dir>/aterm\-<pid>.sock ", " <dir>/aterm\-<pid>.token
An instance's control socket and its per\-launch token;
.I <dir>/aterm.sock
points at the newest.
.TP
.I <logs>/messages.log
Every message aterm showed or recorded (rotated past 1 MiB, one older copy
kept); notices scripts post go to
.IR messages.wire.log .
.I <logs>
is
.B ~/Library/Logs/aterm
(macOS),
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/aterm/logs
(Linux) or
.B %LOCALAPPDATA%\eaterm\elogs
(Windows).
.TP
.I <logs>/session\-log/
The session journal (on by default on macOS and Linux, while
.B restore_session
is on; a headless instance keeps none;
.B "[session_log] journal = false"
turns it off): each session's log as
.IB <instance> . <n> .slog
segments, 0600 in a 0700 folder, the oldest deleted first to keep it near
64 MiB and 2048 files (files a running instance is still writing can hold it
above that), so
.B aterm ctl @<sid> log
and
.B aterm ctl @<sid> brief
answer for a session from an earlier run. It keeps each command's program name
(a secret typed at a prompt and run is kept as that name too; the wrapper's
own name when the wrapper's flags are not known) and, for a listed tool, one
known subcommand word, the session's folder, its meta values (an attention
text only as a fingerprint) and mail's senders and recipients; never a
command's other arguments, an OSC title, a mail's body or an agent's
transcript text. Off, nothing is created there, and nothing already there is
deleted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
aterm                                  # an interactive shell
aterm \-\-sandbox                        # the same shell under the macOS sandbox
aterm \-\-headless \-\-control\-sock /tmp/a.sock \-\-columns 110 \-\-lines 32 &
aterm ctl \-\-sock /tmp/a.sock image before.png
aterm ctl ls                           # every session of every instance
aterm ctl @s\-ab12cd text
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.B aterm help
(the full manual),
.B aterm ctl help
(the control verbs),
.B aterm \-\-window \-\-help
(window flags and config keys), and
.I docs/INTROSPECTION.md
in the aterm source tree.
